# Server + SWR Pattern (all list/data pages)

Every page that renders a list or data-heavy component follows this pattern:

**Server page** (`page.tsx`) — fetches initial data, passes as `fallbackData`:
```tsx
export default async function MembersPage() {
  const user = await requireAuth();
  const profile = await getCurrentUserProfile(user.id);
  await requireTrialAccess(profile.organizationId);
  const members = await getMembers();

  return (
    <AppLayout>
      <MembersListContainer fallbackData={{ members, total: members.length, search: null }} />
    </AppLayout>
  );
}
```

**Client container** — accepts `fallbackData`, uses SWR, shows spinner only when `!data`:
```tsx
export function MembersListContainer({ fallbackData }: Props) {
  const { data, error } = useSWR<MembersResponse>(apiUrl, fetcher, {
    keepPreviousData: true,
    fallbackData: debouncedSearch ? undefined : fallbackData,
  });
  useTrialGuard(error);

  if (!data) return <Spinner />;  // not isLoading — fallbackData populates data
  // ...
}
```

# Rules

- **Never** add `loading.tsx` alongside server pages that use `await` — it shows on every navigation
- Use `!data` (not `isLoading`) for the loading guard when `fallbackData` is provided
- Use `keepPreviousData: true` in SWR to avoid flickering during search/filter changes
- Pass SWR the `fetcher` from `@/lib/fetcher` — it attaches `.info` and `.status` to errors

# User Data Prefetch (layout-level)

The root layout starts `getUserData()` as an unresolved promise and injects it into `SWRConfig`:
```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
const userDataPromise = getUserData(); // no await
<SWRConfig value={{ fallback: { "/api/auth/me": userDataPromise } }}>
```

This means `useUser()` resolves instantly on hydration — never add a loading state for user/trial data in UI components.
